<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Hellenic Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1974 CE – present · capital Athens</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Modern Greece, the parliamentary republic founded in 1974 after the fall of the military junta, a member of the European Union since 1981.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The military junta that had ruled the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece</a> since 1967 collapsed in July 1974 after its coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> provoked a Turkish invasion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantinos-karamanlis">Konstantinos Karamanlis</a> returned from exile to lead a civilian government, a transition known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metapolitefsi">Metapolitefsi</a>. In December 1974 a referendum rejected the monarchy, and a new constitution was adopted in 1975.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> is a parliamentary republic with a president as head of state and a prime minister heading the government; politics long alternated between New Democracy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasok">PASOK</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-papandreou">Andreas Papandreou</a>. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> in 1981 and the euro in 2001, and hosted the 2004 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2004-summer-olympics">Athens Olympics</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> has a constitutional status.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The debt crisis from 2010 brought international bailouts, deep austerity and political upheaval, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriza">Syriza</a> governments of 2015-2019. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> exited its last bailout programme in 2018 and remains a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> as its capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hellenic-republic.jpg" alt="Hellenic Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Greece.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1974 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Athens</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Konstantinos Karamanlis, Andreas Papandreou, Kyriakos Mitsotakis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Third Hellenic Republic, Greece</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=hellenic-republic" aria-label="Hellenic Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5271 812 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hellenic Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5591 1068L5596 994M5591 1068L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5596 994h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Athens. <a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=hellenic-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
